  "title": "Hello!",
  "tip": "**আসসালামু আলাইকুম** (*assalamu alaikum*, 'peace be upon you') is the everyday hello in Bangladesh; the reply is **ওয়ালাইকুম আসসালাম** (*walaikum assalam*). Hindu Bengalis, and most people in Kolkata, say **নমস্কার** (*nomoshkar*) instead. For 'yes', **জি** (*ji*) is the polite choice with elders and strangers; **হ্যাঁ** (*hya~*) is neutral.",
  "vocab": [
    { "id": "assalamu-alaikum", "bn": "আসসালামু আলাইকুম", "tr": "assalamu alaikum", "en": "hello (peace be upon you)", "notes": "The standard greeting in Bangladesh, especially among Muslims. Often shortened to সালাম (salam)." },
    { "id": "walaikum-assalam", "bn": "ওয়ালাইকুম আসসালাম", "tr": "walaikum assalam", "en": "hello (in reply)", "notes": "Reply to আসসালামু আলাইকুম: 'and peace be upon you too'." },
    { "id": "nomoshkar", "bn": "নমস্কার", "tr": "nomoshkar", "en": "hello (Hindu greeting)", "notes": "Used by Hindus in Bangladesh; the usual formal greeting in West Bengal (Kolkata). Said with palms pressed together." },
    { "id": "hya", "bn": "হ্যাঁ", "tr": "hya~", "en": "yes", "notes": "Nasal vowel, like 'hæ' said through the nose." },
    { "id": "ji", "bn": "জি", "tr": "ji", "en": "yes (polite)", "notes": "Respectful 'yes' used widely in Bangladesh; also spelled জ্বি. Rare in Kolkata." },
    { "id": "na", "bn": "না", "tr": "na", "en": "no" },
    { "id": "dhonnobad", "bn": "ধন্যবাদ", "tr": "dhonnobad", "en": "thank you", "notes": "Between close family and friends, Bengalis thank each other less than English speakers do; ধন্যবাদ can sound formal there." }
  ],
